WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.330
Beginning with the first Star Wars film in
1977 and spanning nine full-length motion

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:09.000
pictures, the Skywalker Saga is over 40 years
old.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.270
Between A New Hope and The Rise of Skywalker,
there are still plenty of questions left unanswered.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:18.750
Fair warning: major spoilers for The Rise
of Skywalker and The Mandalorian lie ahead.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.930
In the first entry in the most recent trilogy,
The Force Awakens, we learn the diminutive

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:27.410
Maz Kanata somehow has Luke's first lightsaber
the one Obi-wan Kenobi gives him in A New

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.570
Hope and that originally belonged to his father
Anakin.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.270
Han asks Maz how she got it, and she answers
only:

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.870
"A good question.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.109
For another time."

00:00:37.109 --> 00:00:41.550
Well, whatever "another time" means, it's
outside the Skywalker Saga.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:46.069
The Rise of Skywalker ends with Luke's first
lightsaber alongside Leia's, buried near Luke's

00:00:46.069 --> 00:00:48.010
old home on Tatooine.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.300
It's a question not easily answered.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.280
Luke loses his first lightsaber when Darth
Vader chops off his hand during their climactic

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.730
duel at the end of The Empire Strikes Back,
in Cloud City above the planet Bespin.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.789
Luke's hand and his weapon fall down the same
long shaft Luke drops down himself.

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:07.800
The planet Maz's tavern is on is Takodana
in a completely different system from Bespin.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.619
It isn't unthinkable the saber could find
its way to Maz's hands.

00:01:11.619 --> 00:01:14.110
Presumably someone found it and eventually
sold it to her.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.300
Maybe she found it herself, or maybe she acquired
it through less than legal means.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.490
Whatever path it took, we may never know.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.820
We can't really count The Mandalorian as part
of the Skywalker Saga, but the emergence of

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.640
the Child better known as Baby Yoda is for
now linked to the tiny Jedi Master Yoda who

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.000
appears both in person and through just his
voice in most of the Skywalker Saga films.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.290
Fan theories sprang up almost immediately
upon Baby Yoda's appearance at the end of

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:43.409
The Mandalorian's first episode, with many
guessing the toddler is either a clone of,

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:45.500
or directly related to, Master Yoda.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.399
On one hand it's kind of silly to assume the
child has anything at all to do with Yoda

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:52.270
other than being members of the same species.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.920
After all, if you're introduced to a human
character in a Star Wars film you don't immediately

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.290
assume, "Oh, that must be Han Solo's nephew."

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.810
"Come on, let's keep a little optimism here."

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:05.440
On the other hand, the assumption is understandable
since we know so very little about Yoda and

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.000
the Child's species.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.420
We don't even know their species' name yet,
which George Lucas purposely left out of the

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.420
mythology.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.640
If there is a deeper link between the two,
we'll likely have to wait until further episodes

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.830
of The Mandalorian to learn it, since the
hero himself is now searching for the Child's

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:20.830
planet.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.540
The Skywalker Saga offers no answers, for
now.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:28.110
One of the biggest surprises in the Star Wars
prequels was the discovery that it was the

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.290
young Anakin Skywalker who created C-3PO.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:36.530
The protocol droid's best friend, R2-D2 appears
in all three prequels as well, yet when Obi-wan

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:41.940
is reunited with them in A New Hope he doesn't
seem to recognize them, and we never learn

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.180
why.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.660
"Hello there."

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.700
Of course the real reason is most likely because
The Phantom Menace came out over 20 years

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:54.740
after the first Star Wars film and George
Lucas had no idea in 1977 the droids would

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.630
appear in any prequels.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.460
But there are some possible narrative explanations
too.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.680
It could be that Kenobi does remember the
droids but is keeping that to himself.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.430
"There aren't the droids you're looking for."

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.810
After all, he clearly doesn't want to tell
Luke about his father, and explaining his

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.460
acquaintance to the droids brings him closer
to that difficult truth.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.130
It's also possible he genuinely doesn't remember
them.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:21.820
Kenobi isn't depicted in the earlier films
as being particularly attached to droids.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.370
A story in The Clone Wars shows Kenobi is
not nearly as concerned as Anakin when R2-D2

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.370
is presumed lost.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.550
"Anakin, it's only a droid.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.730
You know attachment is not acceptable for
a Jedi."

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.700
Anakin only convinces his master to let him
rescue the droid by telling him there's sensitive

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.590
military data in its memory.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:43.210
When Finn, Rey, and the other heroes believe
they're sinking to their deaths in The Rise

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:47.360
of Skywalker, Finn tries to tell Rey something
but he never gets the chance.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.430
Thankfully, the heroes survive and when Rey
asks Finn what he was going to tell her, he

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.060
says he'll tell her later.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.590
Poe teases Finn about it, but by the end of
the film we still haven't learned whatever

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:58.340
it was Finn meant to say.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.340
The most obvious assumption is that he was
to going to profess his love for her.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.480
The two obviously care for one another and
shortly after they first meet in The Force

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.730
Awakens Finn asks her pointedly if she has
a boyfriend.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:09.130
"You got a boyfriend?

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.130
Cute boyfriend?"

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.710
"None of your business, that's why."

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:15.960
According to a CinemaBlend report quoting
Rise of Skywalker director and co-writer J.J.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.720
Abrams, however, Finn's motivations may have
been decidedly less romantic.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.690
Rather than making a play for Rey, he was
apparently going to confirm something suggested

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:29.970
repeatedly throughout the sequel trilogy,
namely that he's Force-sensitive.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.790
There are two big hints in The Rise of Skywalker
that Finn might have enough of a connection

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:35.910
with the Force to be trained as a Jedi.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:39.760
First, there's when Finn assures fellow reformed
storm trooper Jannah that it was the Force

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.600
that made it possible for his true self to
emerge from the First Order's conditioning.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.030
"The Force brought us together."

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:50.500
Later, in the battle against the Final Order
fleet, Finn says he knows the Order has transferred

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.280
their navigation from the ground tower to
the command ship only because he can feel

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.280
it.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.680
When we think back to The Force Awakens, there
were hints since the very beginning.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.220
Finn makes a memorable impression on Kylo
Ren after the village is slaughtered in the

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.250
movie's opening.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:07.440
Later, when he escapes with Poe, Kylo knows
which storm trooper he was right away.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.920
"The one from the village.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:11.920
FN-2187."

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.960
Both moments suggest Finn is either causing,
or being affected by, ripples in the Force,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.700
which Kylo feels as well.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.500
Meanwhile, General Hux and Captain Phasma
seem confused since he had shown:

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.870
"No prior signs of nonconformity."

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:28.390
The Rise of Skywalker ends without any firm
word either way, but it seems at least possible

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:33.810
the man formerly known as FN-2187 might be
due a lightsaber of his own.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:38.300
The first time we see a Sith using Force Lightning
is in Return of the Jedi when Emperor Palpatine

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.710
nearly kills Luke Skywalker with it.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:44.450
Since then, it's been clear that some Sith
can use it while others can't.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:48.240
But it isn't really clear what governs who
gets to use it and who doesn't.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.850
We never see Darth Vader using Force Lightning
but considering how much of his body is mechanical,

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:56.010
it's a safe assumption that using it would
be dangerous to him.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:58.320
But we also never see Darth Maul use it.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.610
Supreme Leader Snoke never uses it as far
as we know, though, admittedly, his death

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:06.950
in The Last Jedi doesn't give us much chance
to see what he can or can't do.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:10.450
Kylo Ren doesn't appear to be able to summon
the stuff, and considering his temper and

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:14.550
overall lack of subtlety, we're pretty sure
if he could, he would.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:18.670
One might assume that Sith Masters like Palpatine
purposely refrain from teaching their students

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.070
how to use Force Lightning to make sure they
have an advantage, should the student try

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:23.210
to kill them.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:26.810
But that wouldn't explain how Count Dooku
knows how to use it, nor how Rey, without

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:31.760
even consciously trying, summons Force Lightning
in The Rise of Skywalker.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.400
Even if you missed all the promotional lead-ups
to The Rise of Skywalker mentioning Emperor

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.900
Palpatine's return, his resurrection is made
clear in the beginning of the film.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:44.250
What isn't answered at any point is exactly
how the Sith Master survived his supposed

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.110
death at the end of Return of the Jedi.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:50.520
In Revenge of the Sith, Palpatine says he
knows how to bring the dead back to life and

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:55.300
uses this claim to seduce Anakin Skywalker,
who is plagued by premonitions of Padme's

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:56.550
death, to the dark side.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:00.680
"You could actually save people from death?"

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:05.130
Still, even if we assume Palpatine is telling
Anakin the truth, that doesn't explain how

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:10.210
the Sith Lord can resurrect himself, particularly
when you consider the manner of his death.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:14.560
The dying Vader hurls Palpatine off a catwalk
down a long shaft, and the Emperor appears

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.949
to explode when he reaches the bottom.

00:07:16.949 --> 00:07:21.180
We're not suggesting Palpatine's resurrection
is uniquely unlikely in a narrative so full

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.750
of making the impossible possible, but The
Rise of Skywalker nevertheless fails to explain

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:29.830
exactly how he survives not only his drop
down that shaft but the destruction of the

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:31.990
second Death Star.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:36.050
At the end of The Rise of Skywalker, Rey uses
the strength of all the Jedi before her and

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:39.050
reflects Palpatine's power back at him, killing
him.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.490
But...is he really dead?

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:44.650
Palpatine's death seems pretty final at the
end of The Rise of Skywalker.

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.520
We see him blown to pieces.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:50.620
But arguably his death doesn't seem any more
or less final at the end of Return of the

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:51.620
Jedi.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:55.150
If Palpatine can come back from that, what
can't he come back from?

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:59.330
Further complicating things is the fact that
we're never told exactly how Palpatine survives

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:00.820
Return of the Jedi.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:05.360
If we knew that, we might see that somehow
the same or similar explanation doesn't apply

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:06.360
this time.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:10.150
In the end, it may be best to assume that
we don't know whether or not this is really

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:14.810
the end for Palpatine…or how many times
Palpatine has died, or even which Palpatines

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.389
have been the real Palpatine.

00:08:17.389 --> 00:08:21.639
In spite of the fact that he personally trains
Obi-wan Kenobi, he's the one who finds Anakin

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:25.960
Skywalker, and he's the first Jedi we see
communicating from beyond the grave, Qui-Gon

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.550
Jinn is never mentioned in the original trilogy
or in the more recent films of the Skywalker

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:31.550
Saga.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:35.330
The only teacher Kenobi mentions is Yoda,
and says he took it upon himself to train

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:38.699
Anakin never mentioning that it was Qui-Gon's
dying wish.

00:08:38.699 --> 00:08:41.450
"Promise me you will train the boy."

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:45.660
The real world reason is probably no more
complicated than the fact that George Lucas

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:49.850
hadn't yet conceived of Jinn in the original
trilogy, but no narrative reason has been

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:50.850
offered.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:55.009
One possibility is that, not wanting him to
learn the identity of his father, Kenobi and

00:08:55.009 --> 00:08:58.410
Yoda chose to give Luke as few details as
possible.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.059
It could also be that Jinn's reputation is
spotty.

00:09:01.059 --> 00:09:05.190
After all, he brings Anakin to the Jedi, and
a lot of blood is spilled before Anakin is

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:06.190
redeemed.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:10.600
The Rise of Skywalker finally answers the
question of Rey's parentage.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:14.430
We learn she's the granddaughter of Emperor
Palpatine and that her birth parents sold

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:18.339
her into servitude on Jakku to save her from
the Emperor, after which her parents were

00:09:18.339 --> 00:09:20.350
murdered by a Jedi hunter.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:22.920
The only missing piece is Rey's grandmother.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:27.210
Emperor Palpatine isn't exactly a stunning
paragon of charisma, so it's natural to wonder

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:30.660
exactly who agreed to make a union with the
Sith Lord.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:33.000
The prequels give us one possibility.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.329
Seen often standing next to Palpatine in Attack
of the Clones and Revenge of the Sith is a

00:09:37.329 --> 00:09:40.579
quiet, pale, and eerie-looking bald woman
named Sly Moore.

00:09:40.579 --> 00:09:44.649
The official Star Wars website describes her
as, quote, "Palpatine's senior administrative

00:09:44.649 --> 00:09:48.920
aide during his chancellorship" and says that
rumors surrounded the enigmatic woman.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.050
The expanded universe offers a couple of other
options, though they're from stories no longer

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:54.070
considered canon.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:10:00.170
In the 1995 novel Children of the Jedi by
Barbara Hambly, Roganda Ismaren is introduced.

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:04.800
The former Jedi youngling is captured by the
Empire and made Palpatine's concubine.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:09.300
There's also Ysanne Isard, introduced in the
Dark Horse comic book series X-Wing: Rogue

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:10.300
Squadron.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:14.740
At one point Isard is the Director of Imperial
Intelligence, and actually takes over leadership

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:18.110
of the Empire after Palpatine's "death" in
Return of the Jedi.

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:22.189
Her position suggests she likely at one point
had a close relationship with the Emperor,

00:10:22.189 --> 00:10:25.269
though anything romantic is left to speculation.

